# Real Data Pipeline — Intel Gaming Dashboard

## What This Solves

The dashboard currently uses seeded random generators for all metrics. This plan replaces those generators with **real PTAT Monitor CSV data** processed by a Python ETL script, producing JSON files the React app reads at runtime.

This is **not** a backend rewrite — the dashboard stays a static React SPA. The pipeline runs **once per build drop** and outputs static JSON.

---

## What We Know From The CSV

The existing [FF_Dawntrail_L2C1_PTATMonitor_17-10-2025_16H-14-12S719.csv](file:///c:/code/gaming-dashboard/FF_Dawntrail_L2C1_PTATMonitor_17-10-2025_16H-14-12S719.csv) tells us the exact schema:

| Signal | CSV Column | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| **SKU identity** | `CPU Name` (col 10) | e.g. `"ARL HX (SBGA)"` — encodes program + SKU |
| **Build/FW** | `Firmware Version` (col 9) | e.g. `0x2210072` → maps to build ID |
| **Timestamp** | `Date` + `Relative Time(mS)` | Per-sample timeline (0–60,000ms typical) |
| **IA Power** | `Power-IA Power(Watts)` (col 368) | Core power draw |
| **GT Power** | `Power-GT Power(Watts)` (col 369) | iGPU power draw |
| **IA Clip Reason** | `Turbo Parameters-IA Clip Reason` (col 378) | MAX_TURBO / PBM_PL1 / THERMAL etc. |
| **GT Clip Reason** | `Turbo Parameters-Gt Clip Reason` (col 379) | Discrete vs integrated context |
| **Per-core freq** | `CPU0..23-Frequency(MHz)` | 24 per-core columns |
| **Per-core temp** | `CPU0..23-Temperature(Degree C)` | 24 per-core columns |
| **Per-core type** | `CPU0-Core Type` | `"P Core"` or `"E Core"` |
| **Per-core throttle** | `CPU0-Throttling Reason` | `"No"` / `"Thermal"` / `"Power Limit"` |
| **Pkg C-State** | `Pkg CState Residency-C0/C2/C6/C8/C10(percent)` | Package C-state breakdown |
| **Per-core C-State** | `CPU0..23 CState Residency-C0/C1/C6/C7` | 24 cores × 4 states |
| **iGPU freq** | `Gfx Component-Current Slice-Gfx Frequency(MHz)` | "Invalid" when discrete GPU used |
| **iGPU temp** | `Gfx Component-DTS(Degree C)` | GT die temp |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **FPS / Frame Time data is NOT in the PTAT CSV.** PTAT is a CPU/power telemetry tool. FPS must come from a separate source — PresentMon, FrameView, or Intel PresentMon SDK CSVs. The pipeline design accounts for two input types.

---

## User Review Required

> [!WARNING]
> **Confirm before proceeding:**
> 1. What tool generates your FPS/frame time data? (PresentMon JSON, FrameView CSV, OCAT, something custom?)
> 2. What is your file naming convention — does the filename encode Game + SKU + Build, or is that inside the file headers?
> 3. iGPU vs dGPU: is this a separate column, a separate file, or encoded in the `CPU Name` / filename?
> 4. Arrow Lake has 9 SKUs and Nova Lake has 15 — do you want the dashboard's [programs.js](file:///c:/code/gaming-dashboard/src/data/programs.js) updated to list all of them, or keep it grouped/filtered?

---

## Architecture: Static-First Pipeline

```
📂 data/raw/
   └── 2025.48/                   ← build folder
       └── arl-hx/                ← SKU folder
           ├── ff-dawntrail/      ← game folder
           │   ├── ptat.csv       ← PTAT Monitor (701 cols, ~3870 rows)
           │   └── fps.csv        ← PresentMon / FrameView
           └── cyberpunk/
               ├── ptat.csv
               └── fps.csv

        ↓  python pipeline/process.py

📄 src/data/results.json          ← React reads this
📄 src/data/timeseries.json       ← Raw chart data (frame-by-frame)
```

### Why This Shape?
- **One JSON read at startup** — React fetches `results.json` once; no per-click API calls
- **`timeseries.json` is lazy-loaded** — only fetched when the user opens a Detailed Analysis view
- **The pipeline is idempotent** — running it twice on the same input produces the same output
- **Git-diffable** — JSON files check into the repo; you can diff build-over-build automatically

---

## Proposed Changes

### Python Pipeline (new files)

---

#### [NEW] pipeline/process.py

The main ETL entry point. Usage:
```bash
python pipeline/process.py --build 2025.48 --input data/raw/2025.48/
```

**What it does:**
1. Walks the `data/raw/{build}/` directory tree
2. For each `{sku}/{game}/ptat.csv` → extracts aggregated metrics
3. For each `{sku}/{game}/fps.csv` → extracts FPS percentiles + frame time arrays
4. Merges into a single record keyed by `{buildId}_{skuId}_{gameSlug}`
5. Appends/updates `src/data/results.json`
6. Writes per-game timeseries to `src/data/timeseries/{buildId}_{skuId}_{gameSlug}.json`

---

#### [NEW] pipeline/ptat_parser.py

Parses a single PTAT CSV → aggregated metrics object.

**Extracts:**
- `avgIaPower`, `maxIaPower` — mean/max of `Power-IA Power(Watts)`
- `avgGtPower`, `maxGtPower` — mean/max of `Power-GT Power(Watts)`
- `avgPkgTemp`, `maxPkgTemp` — derived from per-core max temperatures
- `avgPCoreFreqMHz`, `avgECoreFreqMHz` — split by `Core Type` column
- `clipReasonBreakdown` — `{MAX_TURBO: 0.55, PBM_PL1: 0.2, ...}` as fractional time
- `pkgC0Percent`, `pkgC6Percent` — average C-state residency
- `throttlingFlags` — `["Thermal", "Power Limit"]` or `[]`
- `timeseries[]` — array of `{t, iaPower, gtPower, coreFreqs[], coreTemps[], clipReason, c0Pct}` for chart rendering

---

#### [NEW] pipeline/fps_parser.py

Parses FPS source CSV (PresentMon / FrameView format — TBD based on your answer above).

**Extracts:**
- `avgFps`, `1pctLow`, `0p1PctLow`, `maxFps`, `minFps`
- `p95FrameTimeMs`, `p99FrameTimeMs` — frame time percentiles
- `frameTimes[]` — raw array for the FrameTime chart (sampled to 500 points)

---

#### [NEW] pipeline/schema.py

Pydantic models for validation:
```python
class PTATRecord(BaseModel):
    build_id: str
    sku_id: str
    game_slug: str
    avg_ia_power: float
    ...

class FPSRecord(BaseModel):
    avg_fps: float
    one_pct_low: float
    ...
```
Validation runs on every file ingested — schema drift in CSV headers raises a clear error with the filename and offending column.

---

#### [NEW] pipeline/sku_map.py

Maps `CPU Name` strings from PTAT to dashboard SKU IDs:
```python
SKU_MAP = {
    "ARL HX (SBGA)": "arl-hx",
    "ARL S":         "arl-s",
    # ... all 9 ARL + 15 NVL + PTL SKUs
}
```

---

### React Data Layer (modify existing files)

---

#### [MODIFY] src/data/results.json *(new file, generated)*

The output schema mirrors [generateGameMetricsForBuild()](file:///c:/code/gaming-dashboard/src/utils/metrics.js#67-107) exactly so no component changes:
```json
{
  "2025.48_arl-hx_ff-dawntrail": {
    "avgFps": 87,
    "onePercentLow": 61,
    "pointOnePercentLow": 48,
    "maxFps": 112,
    "minFps": 44,
    "avgIaPower": 48.3,
    "maxIaPower": 89.1,
    "avgPCoreFreqMHz": 4923,
    "avgECoreFreqMHz": 3841,
    "avgPkgTemp": 74,
    "maxPkgTemp": 91,
    "clipReasonBreakdown": { "MAX_TURBO": 0.55, "PBM_PL1": 0.20 },
    "pkgC0Percent": 78.2,
    "pkgC6Percent": 0.0,
    "throttling": ["Power Limit"]
  }
}
```

---

#### [MODIFY] src/utils/metrics.js

Add a `getMetrics(gameId, skuId, buildId)` function that:
1. Tries to look up `results.json` first (real data)
2. Falls back to [generateGameMetricsForBuild()](file:///c:/code/gaming-dashboard/src/utils/metrics.js#67-107) if not found (demo/missing data)

This **zero-breaks** the existing UI — games without real data continue to show synthetic values.

---

#### [MODIFY] src/data/programs.js

Expand to all real SKUs:
- Arrow Lake: 9 SKUs (with iGPU/dGPU variants as separate entries or a `gpuConfig` field)
- Nova Lake: 15 SKUs
- Panther Lake: current 2 (or more if needed)

---

#### [MODIFY] src/data/builds.js

Change from hardcoded array to auto-generated from available data in `results.json`:
```js
// Derived from results.json keys at build time
export const builds = ['2025.48', '2025.46', '2025.44', ...];
```

---

#### [MODIFY] src/hooks/useGameData.js *(new hook)*

```js
// Fetches results.json once, memoizes, exposes lookup function
const { getMetrics, timeseries, loading } = useGameData();
```

Replaces direct calls to [generateGameMetricsForBuild()](file:///c:/code/gaming-dashboard/src/utils/metrics.js#67-107) across [GameCard](file:///c:/code/gaming-dashboard/src/components/cards/GameCard.jsx#11-370), [ProgramDashboard](file:///c:/code/gaming-dashboard/src/components/pages/ProgramDashboard.jsx#9-250), [LandingPage](file:///c:/code/gaming-dashboard/src/components/pages/LandingPage.jsx#7-153), [ComparisonPage](file:///c:/code/gaming-dashboard/src/components/comparison/ComparisonPage.jsx#10-233).

